example.paragraph:
Thus $c_{1} = 2$, $c_{2} = 3$, and $\begin{bmatrix}
      \textbf{x}
      \end{bmatrix}_{B} = \begin{bmatrix}
      2 \\
      3
      \end{bmatrix}$. The basis $B$ determines a “coordinate system” on $H$, which can be visualized by the grid shown in Figure 1.

content.paragraph:
Notice that although points in $H$ are also in $\mathbf{\mathbb{R}}^{3}$, they are completely determined by their coordinate vectors, which belong to $\mathbf{\mathbb{R}}^{2}$. The grid on the plane in Figure 1 makes $H$ “look” like $\mathbf{\mathbb{R}}^{2}$. The correspondence $\textbf{x}\mapsto\begin{bmatrix}
      \textbf{x}
      \end{bmatrix}_{B}$ is a one-to-one correspondence between $H$ and $\mathbf{\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ that preserves linear combinations. We call such a correspondence an isomorphism, and we say that $H$ is isomorphic to $\mathbf{\mathbb{R}}^{2}$.﻿

statement.paragraph:
Let $\textbf{b}_{1} = \,\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      3 \\
      0
      \end{array} \right\rbrack,\textbf{b}_{2} = \,\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      {- 1} \\
      2
      \end{array} \right\rbrack,\textbf{w} = \,\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      7 \\
      {- 2}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack,\textbf{x} = \,\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      4 \\
      1
      \end{array} \right\rbrack,$ and $B = \{\textbf{b}_{1},{}\textbf{b}_{2}\}\text{.}$ Use the figure to estimate $\lbrack\textbf{w}\rbrack_{B}$ and $\lbrack\textbf{x}\rbrack_{B}\text{.}$ Confirm your estimate of $\lbrack\textbf{x}\rbrack_{B}$ by using it and $\{\textbf{b}_{1},{}\textbf{b}_{2}\}$ to compute $\mathbf{x}$.

Let $\mathbf{b}_{1} = \begin{bmatrix}
      0 \\
      2
      \end{bmatrix}\mspace{-4mu},\,\mathbf{b}_{2} = \begin{bmatrix}
      2 \\
      1
      \end{bmatrix}\mspace{-4mu},\,\mathbf{x} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      {- 2} \\
      3
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\mspace{-4mu},\,\mathbf{y} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      2 \\
      4
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\mspace{-4mu},\mathbf{z} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      {- 1} \\
      {- 2.5}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\,\mspace{-4mu}\mspace{-4mu},$ and $B = \{\textbf{b}_{1},{}\textbf{b}_{2}\}\text{.}$ Use the figure to estimate ${\lbrack\textbf{x}\rbrack_{B}},\lbrack\textbf{y}\rbrack_{B}\text{,}$ and $\lbrack\textbf{z}\rbrack_{B}\text{.}$ Confirm your estimates of $\lbrack\textbf{y}\rbrack_{B}$ and $\lbrack\textbf{z}\rbrack_{B}$ by using them and $\{\textbf{b}_{1},{}\textbf{b}_{2}\}$ to compute $\mathbf{y}$ and $\mathbf{z}$.
